## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React re-renders a component when its props change. Change is determined by `===` (reference equality), not deep equality. Primitives compare by value, but objects and functions compare by identity — a new reference is created on every render even if the contents are identical.

```jsx
// Every render creates a NEW object/function reference
<Child style={{ color: 'red' }} />          // {} !== {} always
<Child onClick={() => handleClick()} />     // new fn every time
```

This means `Child` re-renders on every parent render even if nothing meaningful changed.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

| Tool | Memoizes | Use when |
|------|----------|----------|
| `React.memo` | A **component** — skips re-render if props haven't changed | Child component is pure and expensive to render |
| `useCallback` | A **function reference** — returns the same function across renders | Passing callbacks as props to memoized children |
| `useMemo` | A **computed value** — recalculates only when deps change | Expensive computation, or stabilizing an object/array passed as prop |

**Critical rule:** `useCallback`/`useMemo` are only useful when the consuming component is wrapped in `React.memo`. Without that, the stable reference doesn't help — the child re-renders anyway.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Every consumer re-renders when the context **value** changes reference. The most common mistake:

```jsx
// BAD: new object every render → all consumers re-render
function Provider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    <UserContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
      {children}
    </UserContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

Fix: memoize the value, or split into separate contexts (data vs. dispatch) so UI updates don't trigger consumers that only need the setter.

```jsx
// GOOD: stable reference unless user actually changes
function Provider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user]);
  return (
    <UserContext.Provider value={value}>
      {children}
    </UserContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

---

### 4. Before / After Example

**Before — `List` re-renders on every `App` render:**

```jsx
function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const filters = { active: true };                   // new ref every render
  const handleSelect = (id) => console.log(id);       // new ref every render

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>+</button>
      <List filters={filters} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

function List({ filters, onSelect }) {
  console.log('List rendered');
  // ...
}
```

**After — `List` only re-renders when its props actually change:**

```jsx
function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const filters = useMemo(() => ({ active: true }), []);   // stable ref
  const handleSelect = useCallback((id) => console.log(id), []); // stable ref

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>+</button>
      <List filters={filters} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

const List = React.memo(function List({ filters, onSelect }) {
  console.log('List rendered');   // only fires if filters or onSelect change
  // ...
});
```

**What changed and why it works:**
- `React.memo` makes `List` opt into shallow-prop comparison
- `useMemo` keeps `filters` pointing to the same object between renders
- `useCallback` keeps `handleSelect` pointing to the same function
- Without `React.memo` on `List`, the other two are pointless

---

**General heuristic:** profile first (React DevTools Profiler), then reach for memoization. Premature memoization adds complexity and has its own overhead — it only pays off when renders are genuinely expensive or frequent.
